WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.110
This isn't the story I expected to tell here.

00:00:02.110 --> 00:00:04.060
And I want to be clear at the very start:

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:08.189
Gruinard Island, on the west coast of Scotland,
is not a tourist destination.

00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:11.850
There's not much to see, and what there is,
you can see it from the mainland.

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:14.280
But if you really do want to get closer,
there is a way.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:17.130
And these days, the government
won't even try to stop you.

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:19.369
While I think Scottish right-to-roam law

00:00:19.369 --> 00:00:21.759
would allow us to land this boat
and have a walk around,

00:00:21.759 --> 00:00:23.539
the owners of the island asked that I don't.

00:00:23.539 --> 00:00:24.539
And that's fair enough.

00:00:24.539 --> 00:00:25.989
One of the reasons I want
to respect that request

00:00:25.989 --> 00:00:31.119
is because there is quite a history of Englishmen
coming up to the Scottish Highlands and, uh,

00:00:31.119 --> 00:00:32.640
acting like we own the place.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:33.990
Gruinard Island here?

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:38.430
In World War 2, this is where Britain
tested its biological weapons.

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:42.150
It was contaminated for decades,
and landing was dangerous and illegal.

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:45.570
It has another unofficial name:
Anthrax Island.

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:47.630
...and traditionally at this point,

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:49.520
television crews go into
the history of the place,

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:52.060
and there'd be a lot of pontificating
about whether Britain

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:56.150
would ever have actually used
lethal anthrax bacteria as a weapon.

00:00:56.150 --> 00:01:00.290
The government has always maintained that
the experiments on Gruinard Island sheep here

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:01.880
were just for defence,

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:03.370
to have data in case we were attacked,

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:06.319
or as an early form of mutually-assured-destruction.

00:01:06.319 --> 00:01:08.920
All the records I found back that up,

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:11.380
but who knows what was said
quietly in private.

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:13.439
Britain did draw up theoretical attack plans.

00:01:13.439 --> 00:01:18.250
That seems abhorrent now.
But in times of war, the laws fall silent.

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:19.250
This video isn't about that.

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:20.259
I'm telling you that story

00:01:20.259 --> 00:01:23.939
so I can tell you something else
I found while researching.

00:01:23.939 --> 00:01:26.900
Because it's often difficult to find
truth about history like this:

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:29.749
so before travelling up here,
I went to look at original documents

00:01:29.749 --> 00:01:31.240
in the National Archives.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:35.170
It's really amazing to me that
researchers can just ask for those.

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:36.170
All the details are there:

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:38.390
how much anthrax the scientists
used in each test,

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:40.229
how many sheep were killed and how,

00:01:40.229 --> 00:01:42.859
the dimensions of the
clouds of anthrax given off,

00:01:42.859 --> 00:01:46.540
and the scientists' comments about
just how effective it was.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:49.979
But that's been well told,
in books, in articles, in all sorts of ways.

00:01:49.979 --> 00:01:51.119
It's been badly told as well,

00:01:51.119 --> 00:01:54.649
like in an overdramatic US TV segment
from 2001, where the journalist

00:01:54.649 --> 00:01:57.530
wears a completely unnecessary
respirator and hazmat suit.

00:01:57.530 --> 00:02:01.060
And he didn't need those
because in the 1980s,

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:02.719
the island was decontaminated.

00:02:02.719 --> 00:02:04.810
Partly because of pressure and
petitions from the public,

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:07.299
and there was also more startling pressure,

00:02:07.299 --> 00:02:11.060
when an anonymous group calling themselves
Dark Harvest Commando

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:15.760
dumped anthrax-contaminated soil outside
one of the English government research labs.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:17.990
The people responsible were never tracked
down.

00:02:17.990 --> 00:02:21.850
And while all that was going on,
modern tourism had become a thing:

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:24.650
in 1982, the government had to deal
with a couple of Europeans on holiday

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:27.690
who just landed on the island
in an inflatable dinghy

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:31.680
and had a walk around, unaware of
the danger that was still there.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:34.080
There's a letter in the archives
from the British Embassy,

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.840
and it's this wonderful bit
of diplomatic writing.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:42.720
And it's among those more modern documents
that I stumbled on a story about Gruinard Island

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:48.060
that I don't think has ever been told,
because in context it's a fairly minor detail.

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:52.000
But out of all the stories about the island,
this is the one that stood out to me.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:53.450
So it's 1986.

00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:54.980
The decontamination was successful,

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:58.140
the government used tonnes and tonnes
of formaldehyde disinfectant,

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:00.730
which killed a few other things
apart from the anthrax,

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:03.960
but the ecosystem was steadily
starting to recover from that.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:06.800
For the government, it all
seemed to be going well.

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:08.610
The press coverage was mostly positive,

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:11.010
they got a lot of attention
for doing a good thing,

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:12.690
for finally cleaning up an old mess.

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:15.320
And of course the public's interested
in the whole thing,

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:19.560
"newly cleaned-up secret wartime
anthrax-contaminated island"

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:21.140
is the sort of thing that
attracts people's attention.

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:25.340
And it was being sold for only £500!

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:26.340
Slight catch there.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:29.100
It was being sold for £500
back to the original owners.

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:30.260
Because the government, correctly,

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:33.650
was honouring the original contract
from the 1940s, which specified that

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:35.200
once the island was safe again,

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:39.340
the folks who'd sold it to the government,
or those people's descendants,

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:41.120
they'd have the option of buying it back.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:43.980
For the same price they sold it for, £500.

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:50.590
It was worth much much more than that,
but £500 was the price, and £500 they'd pay.

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:51.590
What this did not mean

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:53.580
was that there was a bargain-basement
private Scottish Island on sale.

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:57.910
But the trouble is that the press can
sometimes gloss over some of those details.

00:03:57.910 --> 00:04:03.080
And as the decontamination story went out
in newspapers and magazines and TV broadcasts,

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:05.170
several government departments
found themselves dealing with

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:08.390
a rush of letters and telephone calls
from the public,

00:04:08.390 --> 00:04:11.810
all asking if they could buy
the island for £500.

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:15.960
"It has come to my attention that the
island of Gruinard is for sale.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:19.130
"I am interested in making an offer to purchase."

00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:21.400
I realise that getting voice actors
to read the letters out

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:22.900
does make them a bit funny,

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:24.760
but I don't want to mock the
people sending the letters.

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:25.820
They weren't ignorant,

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:29.030
they'd just been told by an
apparently-reliable source

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:31.750
that there was a private Scottish island
available for £500.

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:35.330
I'd ask about that.
I might even try and beat the offer.

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:41.180
"The price offered is £500.
I am prepared to offer £501 immediately."

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:42.690
One woman from Glasgow wrote:

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:46.220
"The fear of anthrax pales
into insignificance to me,

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:50.200
"as I have lived adjacent to a council
rubbish dump for nearly three years."

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:51.870
And it wasn't just British people.

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:54.320
The enquiries came from all over the world.

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:56.280
Germany, Ethiopia, Bahrain,
the UAE, New Zealand,

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:58.639
a couple from homesick Scottish immigrants
to Australia.

00:04:58.639 --> 00:05:00.990
"I often long for the old country."

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:03.320
People wrote to any department
that they thought might be relevant:

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:05.040
the Scottish Office,
the Ministry of Defence.

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:07.590
A man from Australia wrote directly
to the Prime Minister

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:09.070
offering a thousand dollars.

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:11.820
"Please forgive me for writing
directly to you, Mrs Thatcher,

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:15.720
"but I am hoping you will forward this
tender to the correct department."

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:18.560
That one arrived three months
after the first ones.

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:20.220
This took a long time to clear up.

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:21.690
Not all the incoming letters
are in the archives,

00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:23.500
probably not all the replies either.

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:26.540
There are also a couple of
grumpy memos from civil servants

00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:27.919
passing the buck to one another

00:05:27.919 --> 00:05:30.340
about dealing with the letters
and the phone calls.

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:33.610
I do wonder if any of these writers
thought their letter would someday

00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:36.210
end up preserved in a national archive.

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:37.560
This isn't ancient history,

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:39.600
that's why I've blocked out
their personal details.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:41.410
And I love this story.

00:05:41.410 --> 00:05:44.410
Because it shows that misinformation
is not a new thing.

00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:45.940
Misunderstandings are not a new thing.

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:49.700
Even now, if you Google
"who owns Gruinard Island"

00:05:49.700 --> 00:05:53.730
you'll find an article about how
Russian oligarchs want to buy it...

00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:55.700
dated April 1st, 2018.

00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:58.230
April Fools' Day is a curse
and we should abandon it.

00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:01.190
And anyone who's ever been in
the public eye, even briefly,

00:06:01.190 --> 00:06:04.199
knows that one wrong word can
come back to haunt you for years.

00:06:04.199 --> 00:06:06.110
It might not even be your word:

00:06:06.110 --> 00:06:08.470
the press might misquote you,
or take you out of context,

00:06:08.470 --> 00:06:12.410
and somehow the world gets
the impression that, for example,

00:06:12.410 --> 00:06:17.050
for example, you're the person to call
about a surprisingly cheap Scottish island

00:06:17.050 --> 00:06:18.310
that doesn't have anthrax any more.

